{
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Ir (to go)",
      "body": "*Ir* is completely irregular — and one of the most used verbs in Spanish. It's always followed by *a* before a place: *Voy a la escuela. ¿Adónde vas?* (Where are you going?)",
      "table": {
        "head": ["Pronoun", "ir"],
        "rows": [
          ["*yo*", "*voy*"],
          ["*tú*", "*vas*"],
          ["*él / ella / usted*", "*va*"],
          ["*nosotros / nosotras*", "*vamos*"],
          ["*ustedes / ellos / ellas*", "*van*"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Verbs irregular only in the yo form",
      "body": "Several important verbs are regular except for *yo*:",
      "table": {
        "head": ["Verb", "yo", "Other forms"],
        "rows": [
          ["*hacer* (to do / make)", "*hago*", "*haces, hace, hacemos, hacen*"],
          ["*poner* (to put)", "*pongo*", "*pones, pone, ponemos, ponen*"],
          ["*salir* (to go out / leave)", "*salgo*", "*sales, sale, salimos, salen*"],
          ["*traer* (to bring)", "*traigo*", "*traes, trae, traemos, traen*"],
          ["*ver* (to see / watch)", "*veo*", "*ves, ve, vemos, ven*"],
          ["*saber* (to know facts)", "*sé*", "*sabes, sabe, sabemos, saben*"],
          ["*conocer* (to know people/places)", "*conozco*", "*conoces, conoce, conocemos, conocen*"],
          ["*dar* (to give)", "*doy*", "*das, da, damos, dan*"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Tener, venir, decir",
      "body": "These combine an irregular *yo* with a stem change: *tener* → *tengo, tienes, tiene, tenemos, tienen*; *venir* (to come) → *vengo, vienes, viene, venimos, vienen*; *decir* (to say) → *digo, dices, dice, decimos, dicen*."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Saber or conocer?",
      "body": "Both mean \"to know\":\n\n- *Saber*: facts and information, and knowing **how to** do something: *Sé la respuesta.* (I know the answer.) *¿Sabes nadar?* (Can you swim? / Do you know how to swim?)\n- *Conocer*: being familiar with a person, place or thing: *Conozco a tu hermano.* (I know your brother.) *¿Conoces Bogotá?* (Have you been to Bogotá?)\n\nWhen the object of a verb is a specific person, Spanish puts *a* before it: *Conozco a Laura. Veo a mi abuela.*"
    }
  ],
